response:
The issue is that for the first verse (fly), the code currently appends the final "I don't know why she swallowed the fly..." line even though it's already the second line for verse 1. The expected behavior: for verse 1, only the two original lines appear (no duplicate). For verses 2 through 7, the chain is built and then the final line is appended. For verse 8 (horse), only the two lines appear and no chain/final line.

In the current code, the `verse` method appends the final line for *all* non-horse verses, including verse 1, causing duplication. We need to skip appending the final line when `verse == 1` (or `index == 0`).

FoodChain.java
```
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

class FoodChain {

    private static final String[] ANIMALS = {
        "fly", "spider", "bird", "cat", "dog", "goat", "cow", "horse"
    };

    private static final String[] SECOND_LINE = {
        "I don't know why she swallowed the fly. Perhaps she'll die.",
        "It wriggled and jiggled and tickled inside her.",
        "How absurd to swallow a bird!",
        "Imagine that, to swallow a cat!",
        "What a hog, to swallow a dog!",
        "Just opened her throat and swallowed a goat!",
        "I don't know how she swallowed a cow!",
        "She's dead, of course!"
    };

    String verse(int verse) {
        if (verse < 1 || verse > ANIMALS.length) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Verse out of range");
        }
        int index = verse - 1;
        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
        sb.append("I know an old lady who swallowed a ").append(ANIMALS[index]).append(".\n");
        sb.append(SECOND_LINE[index]).append("\n");

        if (verse == ANIMALS.length) {
            // horse: only the two lines above
            return sb.toString().stripTrailing();
        }

        // chain of why she swallowed X to catch Y
        for (int i = index; i > 0; i--) {
            String current = ANIMALS[i];
            String previous = ANIMALS[i - 1];
            sb.append("She swallowed the ").append(current).append(" to catch the ").append(previous);
            if (previous.equals("spider")) {
                sb.append(" that wriggled and jiggled and tickled inside her");
            }
            sb.append(".\n");
        }

        // Only append the recurring final line if this is not verse 1
        // (verse 1 already has it as the second line)
        if (verse != 1) {
            sb.append("I don't know why she swallowed the fly. Perhaps she'll die.");
        }
        return sb.toString();
    }

    String verses(int startVerse, int endVerse) {
        List<String> parts = new ArrayList<>();
        for (int i = startVerse; i <= endVerse; i++) {
            parts.add(verse(i));
        }
        return String.join("\n\n", parts);
    }

}
```
